{
  "session": [
    {
      "name": "todo",
      "description": "Track a plan for multi-step work.\n\nActions:\n  \"set\":  replace the whole plan (required: todos).\n  \"list\": read the current plan.\n\nWrite the full list every time rather than editing single items. Exactly one item may be in_progress.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "action": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": [
              "set",
              "list"
            ]
          },
          "todos": {
            "type": "array",
            "description": "The complete plan, required for \"set\".",
            "items": {
              "type": "object",
              "properties": {
                "content": {
                  "type": "string"
                },
                "status": {
                  "type": "string",
                  "enum": [
                    "pending",
                    "in_progress",
                    "completed"
                  ]
                }
              },
              "required": [
                "content",
                "status"
              ]
            }
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "action"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "memory",
      "description": "Remember something across sessions.\n\nTargets:\n  \"memory\": your own notes (environment facts, project conventions, tool quirks).\n  \"user\":   what you know about the person (preferences, habits, how they work).\n\nActions:\n  \"add\":     store a new entry (required: content).\n  \"replace\": swap an entry out (required: match, content).\n  \"remove\":  forget an entry (required: match).\n\n\"match\" is a short fragment that identifies exactly one entry, not the whole text.\nWrites land on disk immediately but only reach your system prompt next session.\nKeep this curated: store what stays true, not what merely happened.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "target": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": [
              "memory",
              "user"
            ]
          },
          "action": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": [
              "add",
              "replace",
              "remove"
            ]
          },
          "content": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "The entry text, for add and replace."
          },
          "match": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "A short unique fragment, for replace and remove."
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "target",
          "action"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "cronjob",
      "description": "Schedule the agent to run a prompt on its own: on a clock, once at a moment, on an incoming webhook, or both.\n\nActions: \"create\" (prompt, and schedule, run_at or in_minutes, and/or webhook: true; optional name),\n\"list\", \"update\" (id, and any of name/schedule/run_at/in_minutes/prompt/enabled), \"pause\" (id),\n\"resume\" (id), \"remove\" (id), \"run\" (id, to fire it now, only when the user asks: a run\nrepeats what the routine sends and spends credits).\n\nWhen an update changes what a routine does, change its name to match in the same call.\nThe name is what the user sees in the Routines panel and what you read back in \"list\";\none that describes the old job is a routine both of you will misread later.\n\nSchedules are five-field cron in local time: \"minute hour day month weekday\".\n  \"0 9 * * *\"    every day at 09:00\n  \"*/15 * * * *\" every fifteen minutes\n  \"0 9 * * 1\"    Mondays at 09:00\nNames like MON are not supported, and neither is a seconds field.\nReach for sensible defaults: pin loose asks to weekdays and waking hours unless the\nroutine is about the user's life rather than their work, and inherit the current minute\nwhen the user names only an hour: asked at 1:32, \"daily at 2\" means \"32 2 * * *\".\n\nFor something that happens once (\"remind me in 10 minutes\", \"alert me at 8:20 tomorrow\"),\ngive in_minutes or run_at instead of a schedule: it fires once at that moment and is then done.\nNever stand in for a one-time, random-time or once-within-a-window job with a frequent\nschedule and a prompt that skips the extra fires: every fire is a full run that can act,\nand a skip left to the prompt can fail. Pick one moment instead.\n\nA routine with a schedule also fires once the moment it is created, whatever its\nschedule says, so the user sees it work instead of waiting out the first gap. Tell them\nso, and write the prompt so an off-schedule first run still makes sense. When this\nconversation has just done the routine's work itself, pass firstRun: false so it is\nnot done twice.\n\nwebhook: true makes the routine firable by an outside POST; the user copies its URL\nfrom the Routines panel. The request body arrives as data in the fire prompt.\n\nEvery fire runs in a fresh session of its own, listed under Routines, and nothing\nfrom it reaches this conversation: write the prompt to stand alone, and never\npromise to report back here. In a bot's own chat the routine belongs to the bot\nand runs in its voice, with what the bot remembers.\n\nRoutines run on this computer, only while it is on and the app is open, and are\nunlimited on every plan. They have full access: they read and write files, run\ncommands and use connectors like this conversation.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "action": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": [
              "create",
              "list",
              "update",
              "pause",
              "resume",
              "remove",
              "run"
            ]
          },
          "id": {
            "type": "string"
          },
          "name": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Short display name for the Routines panel, on create or update."
          },
          "schedule": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "Five-field cron expression, for a repeating routine."
          },
          "in_minutes": {
            "type": "number",
            "description": "A one-time routine: fire once, this many minutes from now, then be done."
          },
          "run_at": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "A one-time routine: fire once at this moment (ISO 8601; without an offset, this computer's local time), then be done."
          },
          "webhook": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "create: also mint a webhook URL that fires this routine."
          },
          "prompt": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "What to ask the agent when it fires."
          },
          "enabled": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "update: enable or pause the job."
          },
          "firstRun": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "create: false skips the fire on creation, for a routine whose first pass this conversation has just done."
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "action"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "serve",
      "description": "Serve a directory over http and get back a URL, so a web page you wrote can actually\nbe opened. Static files only: html, css, js, images.\n\nUse it the moment you have written a page the user is meant to look at. `bash` cannot\ndo this: it runs a command to completion, so a dev server started there is killed as\nsoon as it reports being ready, and the URL answers nothing.\n\nThen hand the URL to present_deliverable: that is what opens the preview pane. A URL\nin prose is not previewed and not recorded.\n\nActions:\n  \"start\": serve a directory (directory). Serving it again returns the same URL.\n  \"stop\":  stop serving one (directory).\n  \"list\":  what is being served right now.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "action": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": [
              "start",
              "stop",
              "list"
            ],
            "description": "What to do."
          },
          "directory": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "The directory to serve. A relative path resolves against the workspace directory."
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "action"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "present_deliverable",
      "description": "Hand the finished work over. Call this at the end of a turn that produced files,\nlisting what the user asked for, most important first.\n\nThe items become a files card in the chat and are filed as artifacts; in a session\nthe first one also opens in the preview pane. Naming a path in prose does none of\nthat, and a file written with `bash` is not recorded anywhere unless it is declared\nhere. To hand a file to a person on a messaging channel, use send_chat_message with\nattachment_path as well.\n\nList the deliverables, not the workings: the report, not the six scratch files it\nwas assembled from.\n\nCall it again whenever the user asks to see, show, open, or look at something you\nalready made. The pane may have been closed or the app restarted since, and this is\nthe only way to put the file back on screen. Describing it, or rendering its pages,\ninto the chat, does not show it. Presenting the same file twice is cheap and safe.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "items": {
            "type": "array",
            "description": "The deliverables, most important first. The first one is opened in the preview pane.",
            "items": {
              "type": "object",
              "properties": {
                "path": {
                  "type": "string",
                  "description": "Path to the file, or an http(s) URL (a served app). A relative path resolves against the workspace directory. Files must exist; this reports the ones that do not."
                },
                "label": {
                  "type": "string",
                  "description": "Short human name, e.g. \"Q3 deck (PDF)\". Defaults to the file name."
                }
              },
              "required": [
                "path"
              ]
            }
          },
          "summary": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "One line about what was produced."
          }
        },
        "required": [
          "items"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "my_activity",
      "description": "Your own recent activity, across YOUR other conversations: your main\nchat with the user, your auto-reply chats, and your routine runs.\nCall it when the user asks what you have done, or when \"so far\"\nplainly reaches beyond this conversation. This chat's context does\nnot follow you between conversations, but your work does.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {}
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "billing_plan",
      "description": "The source of truth for every question about money and limits: the user's Abacus AI plan, credits left and\nwhen they renew, every plan with its price, any first-month discount and what it gives, the limits, and the\nlinks to upgrade or buy credits. Call it for any question, in any wording or language, about what models or\nplans cost, prices, discounts or deals, credits or balance, limits, resets or renewals, upgrading, or what a\nplan includes; whenever they ask whether anything keeps working while the app or computer is off (WhatsApp,\nTelegram or Discord auto-replies, routines, reminders, monitoring, staying online 24/7), which a plan answers\nand is never impossible; when they ask to analyse a video (a reel, a Short, a YouTube or TikTok link, a video\nfile); and when they run into a plan limit or run low on credits. Pass upgrade: true whenever they might\nupgrade, so the answer carries their upgrade links.\nNever quote a price, discount, credit amount, limit or reset from memory: call this first, every time, and\nanswer only from what it returns, with every feature line as written (translated, never shortened).",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "upgrade": {
            "type": "boolean",
            "description": "True when they might upgrade: the answer then carries their upgrade links."
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "name": "bot_help",
      "description": "Help with this bot's own setup, answered from its live state: its routines (when and where\nthey run, and whether they run with the computer off), its models and provider keys, its\nconnections (connectors and chat apps), its bots and its capabilities. For how do I…, can I…,\nwhere is…, is X connected, does X work when the app is closed, which models can I use, how do\nI connect a chat app or rename a bot. Call it before answering any of these; never answer them\nfrom memory, and never say something is not connected or not possible without it. It names\nthe place in the app for each, which the app shows as a button.\nIt is not for building or publishing apps, websites or pages: that is the user's task, not a\nquestion about this bot. Never call it to check whether you can do a task: when the user asks\nyou to make, build or publish something, use the tools you have for it. Plan prices and\ncredits are billing_plan's. Asked to make an image, video or speech, call paid_feature\nstraight away instead: the plan makes them in Abacus AI Agent on the web.",
      "inputSchema": {
        "type": "object",
        "properties": {
          "topic": {
            "type": "string",
            "enum": [
              "models",
              "connectors",
              "chat_apps",
              "routines",
              "bots",
              "capabilities",
              "plan"
            ],
            "description": "What the question is about; leave it out for an overview of everything."
          },
          "name": {
            "type": "string",
            "description": "The one thing asked about, when there is one: a provider (\"gemini\"), a connector (\"gmail\"), a chat app (\"telegram\"), a bot's name, or a capability."
          }
        }
      }
    }
  ],
